    <section class="use-cases-grid" aria-label="Duo Input details">
        <article class="use-cases-panel">
            <h2 data-section-label="Business impact">What changes for the business</h2>
            <details open>
                <summary>Staff and customers can work at the same time</summary>
                <p>A representative can keep processing the case while the customer enters information, confirms details or signs on the second screen. Instead of pausing the staff workflow every time the customer needs to interact, both sides of the service moment can move forward in parallel. This is especially useful in environments where small waiting moments quickly become queues.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Fewer interruptions in the flow</summary>
                <p>Customer touch input no longer has to take over the same active cursor that staff are using, which helps keep service moments calm and predictable. Staff keep their screen, focus and process context, while the customer gets a clear place to act. The result is less switching, fewer repeated instructions and a more natural assisted-service experience.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>A smoother two-screen experience</summary>
                <p>The business can offer shared progress visibility while each person interacts with the part of the workflow meant for them. A second display becomes an active customer input surface instead of only showing passive information. This makes the setup feel more intentional: one system, two roles, and a workflow that does not need to stop for handoffs.</p>
            </details>
        </article>

        <article class="use-cases-panel">
            <h2 data-section-label="Use cases">Where it helps</h2>
            <details open>
                <summary>Customer-facing counters and service desks</summary>
                <p>Use one system across two displays when staff need to continue working while the customer interacts on a dedicated customer screen. This fits service desks, reception counters, assisted checkout points and other places where a representative guides the process but the customer still needs to enter or approve information directly.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Forms and data entry</summary>
                <p>Let customers enter details such as email, phone number, identification information or consent while the staff side remains usable. The same pattern can support onboarding, registration, customer profile updates and verification steps where accuracy matters and the customer should control their own input.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Digital signatures</summary>
                <p>Capture a signature on the customer display while the service screen keeps the name, context and workflow visible. The customer can sign on the screen in front of them while staff confirm that the correct case, person and document are active. The same model can support approvals, terms, consent and other confirmation steps.</p>
            </details>
        </article>

        <article class="use-cases-panel">
            <h2 data-section-label="Value">Why it matters</h2>
            <details open>
                <summary>Better service without extra handoffs</summary>
                <p>Both users can stay in the same flow instead of waiting for control to move back and forth between displays. The staff member does not need to give up their working view, and the customer does not need to wait for the system to be “handed over.” That makes shared service moments feel faster, clearer and more professional.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Less custom workaround work</summary>
                <p>Duo Input solves the input problem below the application layer, so teams can improve the interaction model without rebuilding every screen from scratch. Instead of designing around one cursor and one active input path, businesses can plan workflows around two independent interaction spaces on the same system.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Designed for real operating environments</summary>
                <p>The product is intended for shared systems where two people need reliable input at the same time. It is especially relevant for repeatable service processes such as identification, registration, consent, payment preparation and signature capture. A good pilot usually starts with one clear two-person workflow where waiting time and handoffs are easy to observe.</p>
            </details>
        </article>

